# fixedbugs, M-binding-fidelity, 2026-08-14 (panel 052).
#
# SYMPTOM: `print(n)` and `print(n.to_str())` printed different numbers for the
# same `u64`. At 18446744073709551615 the first said 18446744073709551615 and
# the second said -1.
#
# CAUSE: two readers of one decision. `abort.rs` chose the runtime entry point
# with an exhaustive match over `IntKind` and a comment explaining that `u64`
# needs `hero_uint_to_str` because 2^64-1 read as signed is -1 — the exact defect
# panel 042 records as fixed. `builtins.rs` chose it again, with
# `_ => "hero_int_to_str"`, and the catch-all swallowed `u64`. So the fix landed
# on one path and not the other, and stayed half-landed for two milestones.
#
# The `_` arm is CLAUDE.md §11's rule read backwards: the fallback was in the
# quiet direction, so a width the table did not know became a wrong number
# instead of a compile error.
#
# FOUND BY: `-Werror=sign-conversion`, added for the FFI, which made the
# emitter's own `uint64_t` argument to an `int64_t` parameter a hard error in a
# three-line program with no FFI in it at all.
#
# FIX: one reader, `builtins::to_str_entry`, exhaustive over `IntKind`. A new
# width now has to answer there, and answering wrongly is a Rust compile error.

function main()
    n: u64 @ 18446744073709551615
    print(n)
    print(n.to_str())
    small: u64 @ 42
    print(small.to_str())
